 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Meng et al. Biasing Sampling
-A variant that addresses the limitations of Dwivedi sampling for thin geometries [S1]. It takes geometric characteristics into account to guide scattering away from the object as quickly as possible [S1]. It proposes two methods: **Closest Points**, which searches for the closest point to the boundary at every scattering vertex to increase escape chances in thin geometry, and **Incident Illumination Sampling**, which chooses light vertices proportional to their emission using importance sampling to improve variance in backlit cases [S1]. Only suitable for high-order scattering and isotropic participating media [S1].
+Meng et al. [S1] proposed variants to address Dwivedi sampling's limitations with thin geometries. **Closest points sampling** searches for the closest point to the boundary at every scattering vertex to increase the chances of escaping thin geometry [S1]. **Incident illumination sampling** chooses light vertices proportional to their emission using importance sampling, specifically improving variance for backlit cases [S1]. These methods are only suitable for high-order scattering and isotropic participating media [S1].
 
 ### Adjoint-guided Tracking
-A future work technique where the probabilities of absorption ($P_a$), scattering ($P_s$), and null collision ($P_n$) are biased based on scene information (e.g., light distribution) to reduce variance [S3]. This includes increasing absorption rates in emissive parts or scattering more in brightly lit regions [S3]. It cites Dwivedi (1982) for advanced zero-variance schemes [S3].
+Adjoint-guided tracking is a future work technique where the probabilities $P_a$ (absorption), $P_s$ (scattering), and $P_n$ (null scattering) in the tracker are biased based on scene information [S3]. This includes increasing absorption rates in emissive parts or scattering more in brightly lit regions to reduce variance [S3]. It cites Dwivedi (1982) for advanced zero-variance schemes [S3].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per scattering event, plus the cost of the probe ray. The probe ray requires additional ray-marching or intersection tests, increasing per-sample cost compared to standard distance sampling [G].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$, as it only requires local geometric information (thickness) [S1].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Leads to lower variance in high-scattering, isotropic media [S1]. Convergence is faster because fewer paths are lost inside the object [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Reduces noise in thick, highly scattering volumes. Simple to implement with slab approximation [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Fails for thin geometries with strong backlight [S1]. Assumes isotropy. Requires accurate thickness estimation [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** High-scattering, isotropic participating media where paths tend to get trapped [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** Thin geometries, anisotropic media, or low-scattering media [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Adds overhead for probe ray casting and slab intersection tests [S2, unofficial]. The MIS combination adds computational cost for evaluating multiple PDFs and weights [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Minimal additional memory for storing slab parameters and MIS weights [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Leads to lower variance in high-scattering, isotropic media [S1]. Significantly improves convergence by reducing lost paths [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Effective for thick, highly scattering volumes where paths deep inside contribute little [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Fails on thin geometries with strong backlighting [S1]. Limited to isotropic media [S1].
+*   **When to use:** High-scattering, isotropic participating media with significant thickness [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** Thin geometries (e.g., ears, leaves) or anisotropic media [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Dwivedi Sampling originates from Dwivedi (1982), who proposed advanced zero-variance schemes for radiation transport [S3]. The method was adapted for computer graphics to bias random walks in participating media [S1][S2, unofficial]. It is cited in [S1] as a method to reduce variance by exploiting the higher contribution of paths near boundaries. The technique is also referenced in [S3] as a precursor to importance sampling techniques in volume rendering.
+Dwivedi sampling originates from Dwivedi (1982), who proposed advanced zero-variance schemes for radiation transport [S3]. The technique was later adapted for computer graphics by Dwivedi et al. (cited as [40] in [S1] and [D’E16] in [S2, unofficial]) to improve subsurface scattering and volumetric path tracing [S1][S2, unofficial]. It builds on earlier biasing techniques such as those by Spanier and Gelbard (1969) and Steen (1966) for biasing probabilities towards increasing absorption rates [S3].
 
